<!-- ════════════════════════════════════ SECTION 6: FINDINGS & RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">06</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
  </div>
  <p class="section-desc">Synthesis of the most critical insights from the 2020–2026 analysis period, organized as actionable findings for policymakers working on subsidy reform, tax design, and price stabilization mechanisms.</p>

  <div class="findings-grid">
    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-number">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="finding-title">Crude-to-Retail Pass-Through Is Near-Total (r &gt; 0.9999)</div>
        <div class="finding-body">The Pearson correlation between weekly Brent Crude and global mean retail petrol is <strong>0.9999986</strong> — effectively a deterministic relationship at the aggregate level. Policy interventions at the country level must explicitly decouple retail pricing from spot crude to have any consumer impact. Automatic pass-through mechanisms leave consumers fully exposed to commodity volatility.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-number">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="finding-title">Subsidy Regimes Create a 387% Price Gap — Stable Across All Years</div>
        <div class="finding-body">High-subsidy countries averaged <strong>$0.71/L petrol vs $3.45/L</strong> in low-subsidy nations — a premium of ~387% that has been constant from 2020 to 2026 despite Brent ranging from $47.97 to $130.00/bbl. This implies fully administered pricing in high-subsidy markets, completely isolating consumers from crude volatility, but at a growing fiscal cost that scales with commodity prices.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-number">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="finding-title">2022 Energy Crisis Caused the Largest Absolute Price Shock</div>
        <div class="finding-body">The 2022 energy crisis pushed global mean petrol to <strong>$2.78/L in April 2022</strong> (monthly peak), a 156% increase from the 2020 COVID trough ($1.09/L in April 2020). Low-subsidy countries absorbed the full shock. The 2020→2021 transition showed the steepest year-over-year change (<strong>+53.5%</strong> in both Brent and retail), driven by post-COVID demand recovery combined with constrained supply.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-number">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="finding-title">Europe and Oceania Lead Global Pricing Due to Fiscal Design</div>
        <div class="finding-body">Europe ($3.70/L) and Oceania ($3.66/L) are <strong>62% and 60% above the global mean</strong> respectively — not because of higher crude costs (same Brent benchmark) but due to high excise taxes and carbon levies. The Middle East ($1.23/L) is 46% below average. A <strong>$2.47/L intra-regional spread</strong> exists between the two extremes, with tax policy as the primary explanatory variable.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-number">05</div>
      <div>
        <div class="finding-title">High-Income Nations Tax Fuel More Aggressively (34.7% vs 21–23%)</div>
        <div class="finding-body">High-income countries apply an average <strong>34.7% tax rate</strong> on fuel, vs 21.1% (middle income) and 22.9% (low income). This reflects mature carbon pricing and fiscal objectives. Policymakers in middle-income nations have fiscal headroom to increase fuel taxation for both revenue and environmental goals without immediately matching high-income peers' burden levels. Low-income nations should prioritize targeted subsidies over broad fuel price suppression.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-number">06</div>
      <div>
        <div class="finding-title">Fixed-Rate Subsidy Programs Cannot Scale Sustainably</div>
        <div class="finding-body">Nations maintaining prices at <strong>$0.016/L (Venezuela)</strong>, <strong>$0.040/L (Iran)</strong>, and <strong>$0.056/L (Libya)</strong> face structurally growing subsidy costs as crude prices rise. With Brent at $123.75/bbl in 2026, the fiscal gap between market-equivalent retail prices and administered prices is the largest in the dataset's history. Policymakers should design targeted cash transfer mechanisms that decouple subsidy delivery from the commodity itself.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-number">07</div>
      <div>
        <div class="finding-title">Regional Spread Has Widened in 2024–2026 vs 2023</div>
        <div class="finding-body">The gap between Europe's petrol average ($4.29/L in 2026) and the Middle East ($1.42/L in 2026) has reached <strong>$2.87/L</strong> — up from $2.43/L in 2020. As crude prices remain elevated in 2024–2026, countries without subsidy buffers see prices rise while administered markets absorb fiscal costs. This divergence is structurally growing unless market-linked pricing or stabilization funds are deployed.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="finding-card">
      <div class="finding-number">08</div>
      <div>
        <div class="finding-title">LPG Represents a Structurally Cheaper Energy Vector</div>
        <div class="finding-body">LPG prices averaged <strong>$1.71/L</strong> over the period — <strong>25% below petrol</strong> and 20% below diesel. The LPG-petrol spread has remained stable across income levels and subsidy regimes. For policymakers designing fuel transition subsidies, incentivizing LPG adoption (particularly in cooking and transport) offers a fiscally lower-cost pathway to energy access that does not require deep price-fixing of motor fuels.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Policy recommendation callout box -->
  <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(79,142,247,0.07) 0%, rgba(79,212,160,0.05) 100%); border: 1px solid rgba(79,142,247,0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 28px 28px 24px; margin-top: 28px;">
    <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin-bottom:12px;">Strategic Policy Recommendations</div>
    <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:16px;font-size:12px;color:var(--text-muted);">
      <div>
        <div style="color:var(--accent);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px;">Subsidy Reform</div>
        Replace blanket fuel price subsidies with direct income transfers or targeted smart-card vouchers. This separates energy affordability from commodity pricing, reduces fiscal exposure to crude volatility, and improves targeting toward vulnerable populations.
      </div>
      <div>
        <div style="color:var(--accent2);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px;">Price Stabilization</div>
        Establish sovereign crude price stabilization funds (drawing when Brent exceeds a ceiling, replenishing when it falls below a floor). This smooths consumer price shocks without requiring permanent administered pricing.
      </div>
      <div>
        <div style="color:var(--accent3);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px;">Tax Calibration</div>
        Middle-income nations should implement graduated fuel tax increases tied to Brent price levels — raising rates when crude is cheap to smooth revenue, with automatic rate reductions during price spikes to protect affordability.
      </div>
      <div>
        <div style="color:var(--accent4);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:4px;">Regional Coordination</div>
        The 346× price range between cheapest and most expensive markets creates significant fuel arbitrage and smuggling incentives. Regional bodies should establish minimum price floors to reduce cross-border leakage, particularly in Africa and the Middle East.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
